# Keycloak Ops — cloudless.gr

Operational playbook for the Keycloak instance at **https://auth.cloudless.gr**. Use the
companion script at `tools/keycloak-cli/keycloak.sh` for everyday commands; use the curl
snippets below when you need raw API access or are debugging the script itself.

## Connection info

| Field             | Value                                                                   |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Base URL          | `https://auth.cloudless.gr`                                             |
| Realm             | `master`                                                                |
| Admin user (SSM)  | `/cloudless/production/KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER`                             |
| Admin pass (SSM)  | `/cloudless/production/KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD` (SecureString)          |
| App client        | `cloudless-app` (public, PKCE)                                          |
| Manager client    | `cloudless-manager` (confidential, service account)                     |
| Token endpoint    | `/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token`                          |
| JWKS endpoint     | `/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/certs`                          |
| OIDC discovery    | `/realms/master/.well-known/openid-configuration`                       |
| Admin API base    | `/admin/realms/master`                                                  |

> **Never** put admin credentials in the file system, env files, or commit history.
> Always fetch them at call time from SSM:
>
> ```bash
> KC_USER=$(aws ssm get-parameter --name /cloudless/production/KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER \
>   --query 'Parameter.Value' --output text)
> KC_PASS=$(aws ssm get-parameter --name /cloudless/production/KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD \
>   --with-decryption --query 'Parameter.Value' --output text)
> ```

## Common operations

### Get an admin access token

```bash
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded" \
  -d "grant_type=password" \
  -d "client_id=admin-cli" \
  -d "username=$KC_USER" \
  -d "password=$KC_PASS" \
  | jq -r .access_token)
```

Tokens last 60 seconds for `admin-cli` by default. The helper script caches one in
`/tmp/.kc-token`.

### List clients

```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/clients" \
  | jq '.[] | {clientId, id, enabled, redirectUris}'
```

### List users (first 100)

```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/users?max=100" \
  | jq '.[] | {id, username, email, enabled, emailVerified}'
```

### List groups

```bash
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/groups" \
  | jq '.[] | {id, name, path}'
```

### Add a user to a group

You need the user UUID and the group UUID — names are not accepted.

```bash
curl -s -X PUT -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/users/$USER_ID/groups/$GROUP_ID"
```

A `204 No Content` response means success.

### Disable a user

```bash
curl -s -X PUT -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/users/$USER_ID" \
  -d '{"enabled": false}'
```

### Update a client's redirect URIs

Fetch first, mutate the `redirectUris` array, then PUT the full representation back.

```bash
CLIENT_UUID=$(curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/clients?clientId=cloudless-app" \
  | jq -r '.[0].id')

curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/clients/$CLIENT_UUID" \
  | jq '.redirectUris = ["https://cloudless.gr/api/auth/callback", "http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback"]' \
  > /tmp/client.json

curl -s -X PUT -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/clients/$CLIENT_UUID" \
  --data-binary @/tmp/client.json
```

### Test JWKS endpoint

```bash
curl -s "https://auth.cloudless.gr/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/certs" \
  | jq '.keys | length'
```

Expect `>= 1`. Two keys is normal during a signing-key rotation window.

### Check OIDC discovery

```bash
curl -s "https://auth.cloudless.gr/realms/master/.well-known/openid-configuration" \
  | jq '{issuer, authorization_endpoint, token_endpoint, jwks_uri}'
```

The `issuer` value must match what every relying party has hard-coded — drift here causes
silent JWT verification failures.

### Trigger an SMTP test email

```bash
curl -s -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/testSMTPConnection" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d "$(curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
        "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master" \
        | jq '.smtpServer')"
```

A `204` is success. Anything else: re-check the SES SMTP creds in SSM
(`/cloudless/production/SES_SMTP_USER`, `/cloudless/production/SES_SMTP_PASSWORD`).

## Troubleshooting playbook

### `Invalid parameter: redirect_uri`

1. Look at the actual `redirect_uri` in the failing browser request (DevTools → Network).
2. Compare it byte-for-byte against the client's `redirectUris`:
   ```bash
   ./tools/keycloak-cli/keycloak.sh clients | jq '.[] | select(.clientId=="cloudless-app")'
   ```
3. Watch for trailing slashes, scheme mismatches (`http` vs `https`), and locale prefixes
   (`/en/api/auth/callback`).
4. If the deployed app uses dynamic locales, add a single wildcard entry **at the path
   level only** (`https://cloudless.gr/*`), never as a bare `*` host.
5. Update with `keycloak.sh client-update-redirect cloudless-app <new-uri>`.

### `User not found`

- Confirm you're hitting the right realm (`master`, not a forgotten dev realm).
- Username lookups are case-sensitive in some setups; try `?email=...` if `?username=...`
  returns `[]`.
- If `users?username=foo` returns `[]` but the user logs in fine, the user is in a
  federated source (e.g. LDAP) and has never been "imported" — call
  `GET /admin/realms/master/users?username=foo&exact=true` to force a sync.

### JWT verification fails in the Next.js app

1. Hit `/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/certs` — confirm `keys[].length >= 1`.
2. Decode the failing JWT (jwt.io) and compare `iss` against the discovery doc's
   `issuer`. Must match exactly, scheme included.
3. Confirm `kid` in the JWT header matches a key in JWKS. If not, the key rotated and the
   client cached the old set — restart the Next.js pod or wait for the JWKS cache TTL.
4. Check that the pod's outbound DNS resolves `auth.cloudless.gr` to the public LB, not to
   a stale internal ClusterIP.

### Admin password rotation

```bash
NEW_PASS=$(openssl rand -base64 32)
aws ssm put-parameter --name /cloudless/production/KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD \
  --value "$NEW_PASS" --type SecureString --overwrite

curl -s -X PUT -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  "https://auth.cloudless.gr/admin/realms/master/users/$ADMIN_USER_ID/reset-password" \
  -d "{\"type\":\"password\",\"value\":\"$NEW_PASS\",\"temporary\":false}"
```

Rotate the SSM value **before** the API call so a failed API call leaves the system in a
known-bad-credential state instead of a known-good-credential one (easier to diagnose).

## Architecture reference

- **Runtime:** Keycloak runs as a k3s Deployment in the `keycloak` namespace on the omv
  cluster (`192.168.1.128`). 1 replica + readiness/liveness probes.
- **Storage:** PostgreSQL StatefulSet in the same namespace, persistent volume on the omv
  ZFS pool.
- **TLS:** Traefik IngressRoute terminates at the edge; auth.cloudless.gr → service
  `keycloak.keycloak.svc:8080`.
- **SMTP:** Outbound through AWS SES (`email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:587`,
  STARTTLS). Credentials in `/cloudless/production/SES_SMTP_*`.
- **JWKS endpoint:** `/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/certs` — public, cached
  by clients for 10 minutes by default.
- **Token endpoint:** `/realms/master/protocol/openid-connect/token` — used by all OIDC
  flows (auth code + PKCE for `cloudless-app`, client credentials for
  `cloudless-manager`).
- **OIDC discovery:** `/realms/master/.well-known/openid-configuration` — single source
  of truth; never hard-code endpoint URLs in client code, derive them from discovery.

